.TH LATTICE-SCAN 1 "November 2026" "lattice-scan 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lattice-scan \- check this host, or a project's declared dependencies, against the CVEs CISA lists as exploited, through Lattice
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lattice-scan
[\fB\-\-cve\fR \fIID\fR]... [\fB\-\-cve\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-discover\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fBconsole\fR|\fBjson\fR|\fBcsv\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-refresh\fR [\fB\-\-inventory\fR]] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-deep\fR] [\fB\-\-again\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\fR] [\fB\-\-debug\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR]
.br
.B lattice-scan project
[\fIPATH\fR...] [\fB\-\-cve\fR \fIID\fR]... [\fB\-\-cve\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-kev\-catalogue\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-read\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fBconsole\fR|\fBcsv\fR|\fBjson\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fILABEL\fR]...
[\fB\-\-color\fR \fBauto\fR|\fBalways\fR|\fBnever\fR] [\fB\-\-width\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-fail\-on\fR \fBnever\fR|\fBin\-range\fR|\fBcandidate\fR]
.br
.B lattice-scan recent
[\fB\-\-period\fR \fBall\fR|\fBdaily\fR|\fBweekly\fR|\fBopen\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR|\fB\-\-daily\fR|\fB\-\-weekly\fR|\fB\-\-open\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-read\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fBconsole\fR|\fBjson\fR|\fBcsv\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-fail\-on\fR \fBnever\fR|\fBin\-range\fR|\fBcandidate\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-critical\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR]
.br
.B lattice-scan enrich
[\fB\-\-cve\fR \fITEXT\fR]... [\fB\-\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]... [\fB\-\-max\-read\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fBconsole\fR|\fBjson\fR|\fBcsv\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-fail\-on\fR \fBnever\fR|\fBexploited\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR]
.br
.B lattice-scan posture
[\fB\-\-deep\fR] [\fB\-\-since\fR last] [\fB\-\-attack\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR console|json] [\fB\-\-json\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR]
.br
.B lattice-scan get\-key
\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-position\fR \fITEXT\fR \fB\-\-company\fR \fITEXT\fR \fB\-\-email\fR \fIADDRESS\fR \fB\-\-purpose\fR \fITEXT\fR [\fB\-\-accept\-terms\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR]
.br
.B lattice-scan
.RB { list | impact " \fICVE\fR" | resolve " \fICVE\fR \fB\-\-status\fR \fISTATUS\fR [\fB\-\-proof\fR \fITEXT\fR] | add | stats | inventory | log }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lattice-scan
asks the Lattice MCP server which CVEs CISA lists as exploited, and compares each with what is on one machine or declared in one project. It prints the evidence: the
product, CISA's dates and required action, NVD's affected version ranges, and the version found. A person reads it and decides. It does not detect incidents, it does not
decide whether a host or project is affected, and it never closes a finding by itself.
.PP
It uses the Python standard library only, needs Python 3.9 or newer, and works without a key. Without a key Lattice allows a small number of reads a day; see
.B LIMITS
below.
.PP
Run with no arguments at all, \fBlattice\-scan\fR does \fBlattice\-scan \-\-discover \-\-weekly\fR: it reads the weekly list of recent CVEs and checks the likely ones against this host. The daily list is the default here because the weekly list is long, and a keyless run that has to look many entries up reaches Lattice's limit. Any argument or subcommand runs what it names.
.PP
There are two modes. The default mode checks the
.B host
it runs on: installed packages, snaps, running container images and the kernel. The
.B project
command checks what a project's files
.B declare
it depends on, and needs no installation on the machine it runs from.
.SH HOST MODE
With one or more
.B \-\-cve
the host's inventory is collected (the hardware, dpkg, rpm or apk packages, snaps, running Docker images and what is inside them, kernel, Node.js) and each id is read from Lattice and matched by product name.
A match is a
.IR candidate ,
not a finding about the host: the report shows the installed version against NVD's ranges and whether the installed package's own Debian changelog names the CVE.
For each candidate the tool also reads one more node from Lattice (no key needed) and prints a priority (fix first, fix soon or read, with its reasons), the EPSS score, CISA's SSVC assessment, the weakness, the start of NVD's description, the ATT&CK techniques the graph links to the CVE (marked likely when inferred), the first unaffected upstream version NVD lists and up to three references. Candidates are printed highest priority first. The priority is the tool's reading of CISA and FIRST fields, not a risk score or a verdict. If Lattice does not give the detail, the candidate is printed without it. A CVE named with \\fB\\-\\-cve\\fR that CISA does not list gets the same block without a priority (NVD's record status, EPSS and SSVC when present, weakness, description, techniques, references), at the cost of one more call.
.PP
The inventory also records the machine itself (manufacturer, product model and version, board, firmware version and date, architecture, CPU, memory and whether it is virtual; serial numbers and UUIDs are never read) and looks inside each local Docker image, so that an Alpine or Debian base image is known by its operating system release and its apk or dpkg package list, and a flaw in musl, busybox or openssl inside an image can become a candidate that names the image. Nothing is started: for each image the tool runs docker image inspect, then docker create with --pull never, --network none and --label io.lattice-scan.run, copies only the OS release file and the package database out with docker cp, and removes the container. Environment variables of an image are not kept except those ending in _VERSION. Images with no OS release file or package database (distroless, scratch) are listed with a note. RPM databases in the sqlite format (Fedora, RHEL 9 and later, Amazon Linux 2023) are read; Berkeley DB and ndb databases are not. A distroless image is read from /var/lib/dpkg/status.d. In a deep inventory the tool also streams docker export once for each image and records the names and versions of Python (dist-info), Node (node_modules package.json), Java (jar file names) and Ruby (gems) packages inside, with a time limit of 180 seconds per image after which the result is marked partial. Each image also shows the other local image it was built on, when that image's layers are the first layers of this one. No registry is contacted. LATTICE_SCAN_IMAGES and LATTICE_SCAN_IMAGES_LANG override the level. Cleanup: the container is removed after each image, also on an error; at the end of the run and when the run is stopped (Ctrl-C or SIGTERM) every container it made is removed; a run killed with SIGKILL leaves at most one never-started container per image being read, which the next run removes if it is older than 15 minutes. Only containers with that label are touched (found with docker container ls --filter label=io.lattice-scan.run --no-trunc). Nothing is written to disk by docker cp or docker export (they are read from a pipe, with 40 MB at most in memory); an rpm database is written to a temporary folder that is removed at once. An image that fails is skipped and named; the others are kept. Images are not opened when less than 1024 MB is free where Docker keeps its data, and a run stops opening images after 30 minutes.
.TP
.BI \-\-cve " ID"
A CVE id to check. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-discover
With no ids, no key: read namiq.io's list of recent exploited CVEs, keep those whose product is installed here, and read only those from Lattice (at most \fB\-\-max\-read\fR). The same engine as \fBrecent\fR. Windows, in UTC calendar days: \fB\-\-daily\fR, \fB\-\-weekly\fR (the default), \fB\-\-monthly\fR (thirty days), \fB\-\-open\fR (older than a week, within thirty days, overdue first).
.TP
.B \-\-exposed
Host scans only: list only the candidates whose package owns a listener on this machine or runs as a service here. The others are counted and still recorded. Each candidate prints an exposure label, a fact read from the local listeners and services: it listens on all interfaces, on loopback only, runs as a service with no listener, is installed with no service found, or sits inside an image. It never says the software is reachable from the internet: NAT, upstream firewalls and cloud rules are unknown here, and without root the firewall rules are not read. Candidates of equal priority are listed with the more exposed first. With \fB\-\-fail\-on exposed\fR the exit status is 1 only for a candidate whose version check is in\-range and that listens on all interfaces.
.TP
.B \-\-discover \-\-all
Match every CISA-listed CVE in the signed exploited index (see \fBlattice\-scan index\fR) against this host, locally, for software that is old. Nothing is read from Lattice. The output is statistics only, in every format: counts by verdict and by the year CISA added them, a count under 3 as <3, and no CVE id or product name. The index must be verified and at most 14 days old: exit 2 if it is refused or stale, 3 if unreachable. \fBrecent \-\-all\fR is unchanged (the thirty-day list).
.TP
.BI \-\-cve\-file " FILE"
A file of CVE ids, one per line. Text after # is a comment.
.TP
.B \-\-discover
Find the exploited CVEs CISA added in the last
.B LATTICE_SCAN_WINDOW
days (default 6). Needs
.BR LATTICE_API_KEY :
graph_search is not keyless.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Collect the inventory again now and show it (totals; \fB\-\-list\fR for everything), then run the scan that would have run without it: the daily list of recent CVEs when no id is given, or the ids named. The findings still open from earlier runs are listed with their saved detail, and every CVE in the table has a detail block. With \fB\-\-inventory\fR the run stops after the inventory: no scan and no network call.
.TP
.B \-\-inventory
With \fB\-\-refresh\fR, only collect and show the inventory. Makes no network call.
.TP
.B \-\-share
With the \fBinventory\fR command: print the host's important software as one block to copy (\fBLATTICE INVENTORY v1\fR, at most 150 lines and 12 KB) and paste into My products in the Lattice Analyst. It holds the kernel, what listens, whatever today's CISA list may concern, the running container images, snaps and server software, each with the reason it is listed, and the firewall and SSH settings. No host name, address, user name, serial or path. It reads namiq.io's public recent list once, so that nothing the list could concern is left out.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-lines " N"
With \fB\-\-share\fR, at most N lines (default and limit 150).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-feed
With \fB\-\-share\fR, do not read namiq.io's recent list. The block then says so and may miss items the list would have kept.
.TP
.B \-\-all " FILE"
With \fB\-\-share\fR, also write every package, snap and image to FILE (mode 0600). It is never printed.
.TP
.B \-\-list
With \fB\-\-refresh\fR, print every installed package, snap and image. Without it the inventory shows totals, the hardware and a summary of the images (by base OS, packages inside them), and the path of inventory.json, which always holds the full lists.
.TP
.B \-\-deep
Collect a deep inventory. The default, quick, takes the hardware, the host's packages and snaps, and opens the images that containers are running (OS release and package list; about 12 seconds for 7 images). Deep also opens every other local image and lists the Python, Node, Java and Ruby packages inside every image by streaming docker export (minutes: about 6 minutes for 24 images, with a limit of 180 seconds per image after which that image is marked partial). Same as LATTICE_SCAN_LEVEL=deep. A deep inventory first prints a rough estimate, then one line per image with its number, percentage and elapsed time, and a summary; a completed one is recorded (deep.json in the records folder). After a quick inventory the tool prints one line on standard error saying how many local images were opened, how long a deep one would take and when one was last done (a repeat is suggested after 30 days). \fB\-\-quiet\fR prints none of these lines.
.TP
.B \-\-again
Read ids that were read before.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Print, on standard error, what is being checked: each part of the host collected (packages, snaps, container image names, kernel) with counts, each project file read with its kind, and for each CVE which one it is (n of N), what CISA lists, and whether anything matched. Standard output is unchanged. On by default.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet\fR, \fB\-q
Print no progress.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Keep this run's records in the default folder even when there is no terminal. Without it, a run with no terminal on standard input or error (an agent, a cron job) and no records folder yet writes its records to a temporary folder that is deleted when the run ends, so nothing is left in the home folder. A folder that already exists is used as before, and so is any folder named by
.BR LATTICE_SCAN_DIR .
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Write every request, response and match decision to stderr and
.IR debug.log .
.TP
.BR list ", " impact " \fICVE\fR, " resolve " \fICVE\fR, " add ", " stats ", " inventory ", " log
Read or record the host's findings. A finding is closed only by
.B resolve
with a status of
.BR verified ,
.B fixed
or
.BR not\-applicable ,
and
.B fixed
and
.B verified
need
.BR \-\-proof .
.TP
.BI "add \-\-ref " REF " \-\-note " TEXT " [\-\-on " DATE ]
Record a finding by hand (a reference and a note).
.TP
.BI "resolve " CVE " \-\-status " STATUS " [\-\-proof " TEXT "] [\-\-on " DATE ]
.I DATE
is YYYY\-MM\-DD; the default is today.
.SH PROJECT MODE
.B lattice-scan project
reads the dependency declarations under each
.I PATH
(default: the current folder), builds an inventory of what they declare, and checks it the same way. It writes no host records, executes nothing and installs nothing.
.SS Files read
.TP
Python
.IR requirements*.txt " and " requirements*.lock
(with \fB\-r\fR includes),
.IR pyproject.toml
(PEP 621, optional groups, dependency groups, build requirements, Poetry).
.TP
JavaScript
.IR package.json ", " package\-lock.json .
.TP
Java
.IR pom.xml
(properties resolved),
.IR build.gradle ", " build.gradle.kts ", " gradle.lockfile ", " gradle/libs.versions.toml .
.TP
Rust
.IR Cargo.toml ", " Cargo.lock .
.TP
C and C++
.IR CMakeLists.txt " and " *.cmake
(find_package, FetchContent_Declare, ExternalProject_Add, CPMAddPackage, pkg_check_modules),
.IR conanfile.txt ", " conanfile.py ", " vcpkg.json .
.TP
Make
.IR Makefile ", " GNUmakefile ", " *.mk
(\fINAME\fR_VERSION = x.y.z, pkg-config constraints, library names from \-l flags, tarball names in download commands).
.TP
Containers
.IR Dockerfile* " (FROM, pip install, apt-get install, tarball names) and " docker\-compose*.yml ", " compose*.yaml " (image:)."
.PP
.IR node_modules ", virtual environments, " target ", build output and " .claude
are skipped. If the search reaches its file limit the report says so.
.SS What counts as a version
Only a version that is written down. A pinned version (\fB==2.14.0\fR, a lockfile entry, \fB=1.4.2\fR in Cargo, a Docker tag with two or more numbers such as
\fB16.2\fR or \fB13.0.1\-security\-01\fR) is compared with NVD's ranges. A range (\fB>=1.0\fR), a caret requirement, a minimum in \fBfind_package\fR, a branch name, \fBlatest\fR
or a major\-only tag (\fB16\fR) is not a version, and the result for it is
.BR undetermined ,
never
.BR outside\-range .
Where a lockfile pins a name, that name's unpinned declarations are left out: the lockfile says what is installed. A \fB\-l\fR link flag names a library, not a version.
.SS Who can scan what
Reading many ids is for a key whose owner has confirmed their email. Lattice reports a key's tier in every answer: \fBtrial\fR until the email is confirmed and the terms accepted,
then \fBtrial_accepted\fR or \fBcommercial\fR.
.TP
.B With a confirmed key
(\fBLATTICE_API_KEY\fR set): \fB\-\-kev\-catalogue\fR works, with up to 200 ids read a run by default.
.TP
.B With a key whose email is not confirmed
\fB\-\-kev\-catalogue\fR is refused (exit 2) after one cheap tier check, with a message to confirm the email from the message Lattice sent; no id is read.
.TP
.B Without a key
\fB\-\-kev\-catalogue\fR is refused (exit 2) before any request is made. Only the CVEs you name are checked: \fB\-\-cve CVE\-2026\-46300,CVE\-2021\-44228\fR (comma separated; the option may be
repeated), or \fB\-\-cve\-file\fR with one or more ids per line. A string that is not a CVE id is refused (exit 2) before anything is asked. At most 20 are read a run without a key (200 with one).
.SS Choosing which ids to read
Lattice looks up by CVE id, not by product, so something has to name the ids. Either name them with
.B \-\-cve
or
.BR \-\-cve\-file ,
or (with a confirmed key) give
.BI \-\-kev\-catalogue " FILE"
(CISA's known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json, or one JSON object per line with source_id, vendor_project and product). The catalogue is read locally and only chooses ids:
an id is read from Lattice only when its product is the same as a declared package. Each id is read once per run, however many folders match it.
.PP
Matching is by identity. The product must be the package, its npm scope, or vendor and product together; a prefix counts when the shorter name has six or more characters and covers
70% of the longer (postgres, PostgreSQL). A generic one\-word product (Core, Client) also needs its vendor in the name. For Maven, Cargo, Conan, vcpkg, CMake and Make names the
product as one whole token of a longer coordinate matches too (\fBorg.apache.logging.log4j:log4j\-core\fR is Log4j2) and its versions are compared. A match through an npm scope
alone (\fB@vitejs/plugin\-react\fR for Vite) is a lead only: that package's version is not the product's, so no range is compared and the result is \fBlead\fR. So is a client library that carries the server's name (the PyPI \fBminio\fR is not the MinIO server).
.SS Options
.TP
.BI \-\-cve " ID[,ID...]" ", " \-\-cve\-file " FILE"
Ids to read, as in host mode. Several may be given in one argument, separated by commas.
.TP
.BI \-\-kev\-catalogue " FILE"
CISA's catalogue, used locally to choose ids. Needs a key whose email is confirmed (see \fBWho can scan what\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-all
With \fB\-\-discover\fR: the whole signed exploited index matched against the dependencies the folders declare. Counts by verdict and year only, no CVE id or product name; nothing is read from Lattice. Same index rules as the host form.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-read " N"
Most ids to read from Lattice per run (default 200 with a key, 20 without). Ids over the limit are listed as not read.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " " console | csv | json
What to print. The default,
.BR console ,
is a report for a person. See
.BR "OUTPUT FORMATS" .
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " FILE"
Also write the findings as CSV to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-json " FILE"
Also write the full result as JSON to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-name " LABEL"
A label for each folder, in order (default: the folder's name). It is the
.B project
column in CSV.
.TP
.BR \-\-color " " auto | always | never
Colour the console report. \fBauto\fR colours only when standard output is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset and
.B TERM
is not dumb.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " N"
Console report width (default: the terminal's, between 72 and 160).
.TP
.BR \-\-fail\-on " " never | in\-range | candidate
Exit 1 when a candidate, or an in\-range one, is found. The default is
.BR never :
a person reads the report.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.SS console
A report in four parts: a summary table (files read, components declared, how many are pinned, candidates, and a count for each verdict, with a total row for several projects);
one section per project, each finding showing the declared component with file and line, CISA's dates ("listed ... 3 days ago, due ... in 18 days"), the version check, and CISA's required
action; the ids that were not read; and a legend of what each verdict means.
.SS csv
One row per finding and declared component, UTF\-8, LF line ends, a header row even when there are no findings, columns in this fixed order:
.PP
.nf
project  cve  title  verdict  component  ecosystem  declared  version  file  line
listed  due  due_status  action  why
.fi
.PP
.B verdict
is one of
.BR in\-range ", " undetermined ", " outside\-range ", " lead ", " not\-read .
Rows are ordered by project, then verdict (in\-range first, not\-read last), then CVE.
.B declared
is the text as written (\fB==2.14.0\fR);
.B version
is the pinned version alone, empty when there is none;
.B line
is empty when the file has no meaningful line (a lockfile or a pom).
.B due_status
is "due in 18 days", "due today" or "overdue by 4 days". An id that was not read has an empty component and a
.B why
saying so.
.PP
Text that came from outside (a CVE title, CISA's action, a file name) can start with \fB=\fR, \fB+\fR, \fB\-\fR or \fB@\fR, which a spreadsheet would run as a formula. Such a cell is written with
one leading apostrophe. A program that wants the raw text strips one leading apostrophe; the
.B version
column is already clean.
.SS json
The whole result, one object per project: files read, counts, every candidate with its declared components and version check, the ids not read and why. The same data as the other two formats.
.SH VERDICTS
.TP
.B in\-range
A declared, pinned version lies inside a range NVD lists as affected. Check what is really deployed: a distribution can fix a CVE under an unchanged version number, and a lockfile may not be
what runs.
.TP
.B undetermined
The declaration is a range, a minimum, a floating tag or has no version; or NVD's bounds are dates or build ids; or Lattice listed no range for this product. It could not be judged.
.TP
.B lead
The declared package only shares a scope or name with the product, or is its client library. Its version says nothing about the product, so no range was compared.
.TP
.B outside\-range
Every pinned version declared lies outside every usable range. This does not close a finding.
.TP
.B not\-read
An id that matches a dependency by name was not read from Lattice (the read limit, or a stop). It is neither safe nor affected.
.SH POSTURE
.B lattice\-scan posture
shows what this host exposes and runs, beyond what is installed. It only reads; it changes, kills and installs nothing, and nothing it finds is sent anywhere. It reports signals for a person to read. A signal is not a verdict, and each one says why it can be harmless; the tool does not detect incidents or decide that a host is compromised.
.PP
The scope follows the user who runs it, and the report says which. As root it reads the firewall rules (ufw status, nft list ruleset, iptables \-S), the effective SSH settings (sshd \-T) and every process. As anyone else it reads what that user may, and lists under NOT READ what it could not, so that a missing answer is never taken for a clean one. It never asks for root and never runs sudo; the person decides.
.PP
Quick (default) reads: the listening TCP and UDP sockets from /proc/net with their bind (all interfaces, loopback only, one address), the process that owns each and the package that owns that program, or the container image; the host firewall (ufw, firewalld, nftables, iptables); the SSH server settings from sshd_config and sshd_config.d (the first value of a keyword wins, Include is followed, a Match block is not applied, an unset keyword takes OpenSSH's default); and the running systemd services with the program each runs and whether a package owns it.
.PP
.B \-\-deep
also reads every process it may: where its program lives, whether a package owns it (dpkg file lists, an apk database or rpm \-qf), whether the program was deleted while it runs, runs from memory (memfd) or from a temporary folder, and, for those no package owns, its outbound connections. A program replaced by a package update after the process started is reported as a restart note, not as suspicious. It also reads persistence points: /etc/ld.so.preload, accounts other than root with user id 0, cron files and setuid files that no package owns, and the number of systemd timers. Command line arguments are never recorded, because they carry passwords. Programs in containers, snaps and flatpaks are attributed to those, not flagged. Deep takes about a minute.
.TP
.B \-\-deep
Also processes and persistence points (see above).
.TP
.B \-\-attack
Name the ATT&CK techniques each signal relates to. The table is one signed file that namiq.io publishes beside the exploited index (same pinned key; sha256 and signature are checked, a rolled\-back generation is refused); it is downloaded, not built into the tool, so the tool and the server cannot disagree. Only that public file is fetched; nothing about this host is sent. A technique is a reason to read the signal, not a sign that an attack happened. Exit 2 if the table is refused, 3 if it cannot be read and none is cached.
.TP
.B \-\-since last
Also print what is new, changed or gone since the last stored run: listening ports, running services, effective SSH settings (root only), persistence entries (deep only) and open findings, with the day a new finding was first found. A part read in only one of the two runs is reported as not compared, never as gone. This run is stored for the next comparison. Every host scan does the same at its end. Snapshots are kept in the private records folder (mode 0600), never sent anywhere, and the oldest beyond 30 are deleted.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " console" | json
What to print. The default is a report for a person.
.TP
.BI \-\-json " FILE"
Also write the full result to FILE, mode 0600. It names this host's processes: do not share it.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Print no progress.
.PP
Windows and macOS are not covered. A hostile process can hide from any tool that runs as the same user it runs as; this is a first look, not a replacement for monitoring.
.SH STARTING WITHOUT A CVE ID
.SS recent
Reads two lists published at namiq.io, one of CVEs CISA lists as exploited and one of CVEs scored critical and not known to be exploited, for a period: \fBall\fR (everything in the list: the weekly and the open parts; the default), \fBdaily\fR (added or published in the last day or two), \fBweekly\fR (the last seven days, which includes daily) or \fBopen\fR (exploited entries CISA added before the weekly window but within the last 30 days, overdue first, where overdue means that CISA's due date has passed and says nothing about any machine; it has no critical part).
The list's schema is lattice\-recent/N. This tool reads version 2 and later, so a list that only adds fields keeps working; \fBopen\fR needs version 3.
The request carries no id and nothing about this host. Each exploited entry is then compared with this host's inventory, locally, by product name. Only the entries that may match are read from Lattice, by exact id, and shown like a host check.
The others are listed as \fBunmatch\fR and are not sent to Lattice. A critical entry that may match is shown as \fBWATCH\fR: it is not known to be exploited, so Lattice is not asked about it.
When nothing matches, nothing is sent to Lattice. That is not the same as safe: the lists hold only what was recently added; to check a CVE you know of, use \fB\-\-cve\fR.
\fB\-\-max\-read\fR limits how many exploited entries are considered (default 200, the whole list); \fB\-\-no\-critical\fR leaves out the critical list.
.SS enrich
Takes CVE ids from \fB\-\-cve\fR (any text containing ids), from \fB\-\-file\fR, or from standard input when neither is given (an audit report, an advisory, a ticket), reads each once from Lattice by exact id, and prints for each: whether CISA lists it as exploited, since when, the patch due date, the CVSS score and whether CISA records ransomware use.
It does not compare with this host and writes no records. Without a key it reads at most 20 ids a run (\fB\-\-max\-read\fR changes this); ids not read are listed and the exit status is 3. \fB\-\-fail\-on exploited\fR exits 1 when any id is exploited.
.SS get\-key
Asks for a Lattice key for a real person. Without \fB\-\-accept\-terms\fR nothing is sent: it prints who the key would be for, where the beta terms are, and stops with status 2. The person must have read the terms and agreed, and must read the mailbox given: the key and a confirmation email are sent there, and a click on "not me" withdraws the key.
The terms version is read from the key service's own published description and sent exactly. The key is stored in the records folder as the file \fBkey\fR (mode 0600), never printed, and used automatically by later runs while the file stays private to the user. If a key is already stored it asks for nothing more unless \fB\-\-force\fR is given.
.SH RESULT AS DATA
\fB\-\-format json\fR or \fB\-\-format csv\fR on the host check, \fBrecent\fR and \fBenrich\fR prints the RESULT BY CVE table as one JSON document (schema \fBlattice\-scan\-result/1\fR: tool, command, window, exit code, counts by result, rows, and the findings still open from earlier runs) or as CSV with a header row, and nothing else on standard output; progress and errors stay on standard error. \fB\-\-json FILE\fR writes the JSON to a file (mode 0600, because it names this host's software) and leaves the screen unchanged. A CSV cell that starts with = + \- or @ has a leading apostrophe so a spreadsheet does not run it. \fBproject\fR and \fBposture\fR have their own \fB\-\-format\fR and \fB\-\-json\fR.
.SH THE SIGNED EXPLOITED INDEX
\fBlattice\-scan index\fR [\fB\-\-refresh\fR] [\fB\-\-require\-fresh\fR] fetches, checks and shows the signed exploited index that namiq.io publishes: every CVE CISA lists as exploited, with the affected version ranges NVD gives for it. It is the data a later scan matches against this machine's own inventory, so the inventory never has to leave the machine. The request carries nothing from this machine. The file is checked on its exact bytes: its sha256 against the manifest, then its Ed25519 signature against a key pinned in this program; the generation, schema and as\-of date are read from the signed file, never from the manifest. A copy under a day old is used without a request; a newer generation is downloaded; a lower generation than one already accepted, a bad signature, a key this version does not know (upgrade lattice\-scan) or a damaged file is refused and the previous copy is kept. When namiq.io cannot be reached the cached copy is used while it is within 14 days of its as\-of date. \fB\-\-refresh\fR asks now; \fB\-\-require\-fresh\fR exits 2 if the index is older than 14 days. Exit status: 0 ok, 2 refused, 3 no usable copy.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The run finished. (Without \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR this does not mean nothing was found.)
.TP
.B 1
\fB\-\-fail\-on\fR matched (the host scan, \fBrecent\fR, \fBproject\fR and \fBenrich\fR have it). In earlier versions a host check returned 1 when Lattice stopped it; that is now 3.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, or an input could not be read (a folder, a catalogue, an id file), or the catalogue was asked for without a key, or with a key whose email is not confirmed.
.TP
.B 3
\fBproject\fR or \fBenrich\fR stopped part\-way, or hit the read limit, because Lattice's allowance ran out or it could not be reached. The ids not read are listed; the report is otherwise complete.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LATTICE_API_KEY
A Lattice key. Raises the daily allowance and enables \fB\-\-discover\fR. Never written to any record or log. It is refused over plain http to anything but this machine.
.TP
.B LATTICE_URL
The Lattice MCP endpoint (default https://lattice.namiq.io/mcp).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_DIR
Where host records go (default ~/.lattice-scan). Set it to a folder the user can write when running as an agent in a sandbox. Project mode writes nothing there.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_KEEP
Set to 1 for \fB\-\-keep\fR.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_WINDOW
Days that \fB\-\-discover\fR looks back (default 6).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_IMAGES
Which images are opened, overriding the level: all, running (images a container is using; the quick default) or off (names of running images only).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_LEVEL
quick (default) or deep, the same as --deep.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_MIN_FREE_MB
Free space needed where Docker keeps its data before images are opened (default 1024). Below it the image names are listed and none is opened.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_DEEP_SECONDS
Seconds after which no further image is opened (default 1800). Images not opened are named.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_DEEP_CACHE
on (default) or off. A complete deep scan is kept in the records folder (deep-images.json); a later quick run uses it for every local image whose id is unchanged, instead of opening that image again. off, or \-\-refresh, opens them again.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_DEEP_CACHE_DAYS
How old a kept deep scan may be before it is not used (default 30).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_READ_CACHE
on (default) or off. An answer Lattice gave to a lookup of one CVE id, or to the detail read of one node, is kept in the user's cache folder (read\-cache.json) and reused for a few hours, so a repeat run asks Lattice nothing. It holds graph data about public CVEs only, nothing about this host. off, \-\-again and \-\-refresh ask Lattice again.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_READ_CACHE_HOURS
How old a kept answer may be before it is not used (default 6).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_READ_CACHE_FILE
Where the read cache is kept (default: read\-cache.json in the user's cache folder).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_STALE_MINUTES
Age after which a leftover scan container from an earlier, killed run is removed (default 15).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_IMAGES_LANG
Which images get the Python, Node, Java and Ruby package scan, overriding the level: all (the deep default), running or off (the quick default).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_OBSERVATIONS_URL
Where \fBposture \-\-attack\fR reads the observation table (default https://namiq.io/lattice/observations.json).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_OBSERVATIONS_MANIFEST_URL
Where it reads that table's manifest (default https://namiq.io/lattice/observations.manifest.json).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_POSTURE
A JSON posture report to use for the exposure labels instead of reading listeners and services (tests, other tools).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_INVENTORY
A JSON inventory to use instead of collecting one (tests, other tools).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_PROGRESS
Progress is on by default. Set to 0 to turn it off, like \fB\-\-quiet\fR.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_FEED
Where \fBrecent\fR reads the lists (default https://namiq.io/lattice/recent.json).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_INDEX_URL
Where \fBindex\fR reads the signed exploited index (default https://namiq.io/lattice/exploited-index.json). The signature is still checked against the pinned key, so another address cannot substitute its own data.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_INDEX_MANIFEST_URL
Where \fBindex\fR reads the manifest beside it (default https://namiq.io/lattice/exploited-index.manifest.json).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_KEY_URL
The key service \fBget\-key\fR asks (default https://namiq.io/api/v1/lattice/agent-keys).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_TERMS_URL
The description of the key service from which \fBget\-key\fR reads the current terms version (default https://namiq.io/lattice/openapi.json).
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_PACE_FILE
Where the times of recent keyless calls are remembered (default ~/.cache/lattice\-scan/pace.json, under XDG_CACHE_HOME if that is set). Times only, no names.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_DEBUG
Set to 1 for \fB\-\-debug\fR.
.TP
.B LATTICE_SCAN_TODAY
Pretend today is this YYYY\-MM\-DD (tests, reproducing a report).
.PP
The first internal version read \fBHOST_SCAN_\fR\fIname\fR for each \fBLATTICE_SCAN_\fR\fIname\fR; those are still honoured when the new name is unset.
.TP
.BR NO_COLOR ", " TERM
Respected by \fB\-\-color auto\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.lattice-scan/
Host mode and \fBget\-key\fR, mode 0700, every file 0600: findings.jsonl, runs.jsonl, scan.log, inventory.json and inventory.log, seen.json, debug.log. They name this host's software. Do not commit, publish or share them.
The file \fBkey\fR (mode 0600), written by \fBget\-key\fR, holds a Lattice key; it is read only while no one else can read it. \fBenrich\fR and \fBproject\fR write nothing there.
If the folder cannot be created and \fBLATTICE_SCAN_DIR\fR was not set, the run says so on one line and uses a temporary folder. If \fBLATTICE_SCAN_DIR\fR was set and cannot be used, the run stops with exit status 1 and one line naming it. \fBget\-key\fR never uses a temporary folder, because the key must outlive the run.
.SH LIMITS
Without a key Lattice allows a small number of calls a minute, hour and day, and says what they are in every answer (the access object). lattice\-scan remembers the per\-minute figure the server last reported (5 until it has been told otherwise) and keeps the times of its recent calls (in the user's cache folder, not in the records), so separate runs one after another space themselves out and stay under it; it prints a line when it waits. If Lattice still answers "too many requests, retry after N seconds" with N about a minute or less, it waits that long and tries again, twice at most. A run that reaches the hourly or daily limit stops, lists what it did not read, and exits 3.
A key is for a real person (https://namiq.io/lattice/docs). The tool asks for one only through \fBget\-key\fR, with the person's own details and only after \fB\-\-accept\-terms\fR; it never opens a link from a reply.
.SH WHAT LEAVES THE MACHINE
CVE ids, the node ids Lattice returned for them, and the key if one is set. Package names, versions, file names and the inventory are never sent; matching happens locally.
\fBrecent\fR downloads a public list: that request carries no id and nothing about this host. \fBget\-key\fR sends the name, position, company, email address and purpose it is given, after \fB\-\-accept\-terms\fR, to the key service.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a host for two CVEs:
.PP
.nf
lattice-scan --cve CVE-2021-44228 --cve CVE-2026-46300
.fi
.PP
Check a project for two named CVEs, no key needed:
.PP
.nf
lattice-scan project . --cve CVE-2026-46300,CVE-2021-44228
.fi
.PP
With no ids and no key, check against the recent exploited list (monthly by default; \fB\-\-daily\fR, \fB\-\-weekly\fR, \fB\-\-monthly\fR and \fB\-\-open\fR narrow or change the window). The list is a plain request that carries nothing from your project; only the ids whose product a dependency matches are read from Lattice:
.PP
.nf
lattice-scan project . \-\-discover
.fi
.PP
With a key whose email is confirmed, let CISA's catalogue choose the ids:
.PP
.nf
LATTICE_API_KEY=... lattice-scan project . --kev-catalogue known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json
.fi
.PP
Several folders, labelled, with a CSV for a spreadsheet and a non\-zero exit if a declared version is in range:
.PP
.nf
lattice-scan project api/ web/ --name API --name WEB \e
    --kev-catalogue kev.json --csv findings.csv --fail-on in-range
.fi
.PP
Pipe CSV to a script, reading columns by name:
.PP
.nf
lattice-scan project . --kev-catalogue kev.json --format csv | \e
    python3 \-c 'import csv,sys; [print(r["cve"], r["component"]) for r in csv.DictReader(sys.stdin)
                  if r["verdict"]=="in-range"]'
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Version ranges are compared as upstream numbers. A distribution can fix a CVE under an unchanged version, so \fBin\-range\fR is a lead, not a finding. Name matching is by identity but is
still only a name: the Python package \fBminio\fR (a client library) matches the CISA entry for the MinIO server, and reading the finding tells you which it is. Docker tags that float, ranges
and lockfile\-less projects give \fBundetermined\fR, not a clean bill. An id that is not in the graph's CISA list is "not listed in this snapshot", which is not the same as safe. Tested on
Debian\-family hosts; rpm and apk inventories are collected but matching there is untested.
.SH REPORTING PROBLEMS
A wrong match, a crash, or anything that looks like a security problem: open an issue at https://github.com/namiq\-ai/lattice\-scan, or write to contact@namiq.io for anything you would
rather not post publicly.
